          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with diarrhoea continues to pass large-volume watery stools even after</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with diarrhoea continues to pass large-volume watery stools even after 24 hours of complete fasting. Which mechanism of diarrhoea does this finding indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osmotic diarrhoea</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Osmotic diarrhoea stops when the patient fasts, because the unabsorbed solute (e.g. lactose) is no longer entering the gut to hold water by osmosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Secretory diarrhoea</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In secretory diarrhoea, a toxin such as cholera toxin activates chloride channels, driving fluid secretion independent of oral intake — hence it persists with fasting and the stool osmotic gap is low.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Malabsorptive diarrhoea</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Malabsorptive diarrhoea produces pale, greasy, floating stools (steatorrhoea) with weight loss and nutritional deficiencies, not simply large-volume watery output.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inflammatory diarrhoea</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Inflammatory diarrhoea is characterised by blood, mucus, fever, and tenesmus — signs of mucosal invasion — rather than painless watery stools.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key clue is fasting behaviour. Secretory diarrhoea persists with fasting (toxin-driven chloride secretion is independent of luminal contents), while osmotic diarrhoea stops. Review the four-mechanism framework before proceeding."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The persistence of watery stools despite fasting is the hallmark of secretory diarrhoea, distinguishing it from osmotic diarrhoea where symptoms resolve with fasting."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
