        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 402.85,
            "to": 412.85
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with frequent small-volume stools containing blood and mucus,</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with frequent small-volume stools containing blood and mucus, accompanied by tenesmus. Based on stool characteristics, which part of the gastrointestinal tract is the most likely site of pathology?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Duodenum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Duodenal pathology typically produces large-volume output or upper GI bleeding (melaena), not small frequent bloody stools with tenesmus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Jejunum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Small intestinal pathology produces large-volume watery or fatty stools, not the small-volume, mucoid pattern described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ileum</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ileal disease tends to produce large-volume stools, sometimes with fat malabsorption. Tenesmus is a symptom of rectal and distal colonic involvement.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Large bowel</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Small-volume, frequent stools with blood, mucus, and tenesmus indicate colonic pathology. Stool volume is a clinical localiser: large-volume equals small bowel; small and frequent equals large bowel.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the volume rule from the history-taking framework: large-volume watery output localises to the small bowel, while small frequent stools with blood and mucus localise to the large bowel."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Stool volume is a simple but powerful localiser — small frequent bloody stools with tenesmus point to large bowel pathology, guiding your differential and examination."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
                "type": "auto",
