          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 30-year-old man presents with bloody diarrhoea and fever. His complete blood c</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 30-year-old man presents with bloody diarrhoea and fever. His complete blood count shows neither leucopenia nor eosinophilia. Stool microscopy reveals abundant faecal leucocytes. Which diagnosis is most consistent with these findings?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cholera</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cholera produces large-volume, rice-water stools without blood or faecal leucocytes. It is a secretory process driven by toxin, not mucosal invasion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Shigella dysentery</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Bloody stools with fever and abundant faecal leucocytes indicate mucosal invasion, characteristic of Shigella. The normal white cell differential excludes enteric fever (leucopenia) and helminths (eosinophilia).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Helminthic infection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Helminthic infections characteristically produce eosinophilia on CBC, which is absent in this patient. They also rarely cause acute bloody diarrhoea with faecal leucocytes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Enteric fever</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Enteric fever characteristically shows leucopenia with relative lymphocytosis on CBC. Additionally, enteric fever typically presents with pea-soup stools, not overt bloody dysentery in its early phase.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Use the CBC and stool findings together. Faecal leucocytes indicate invasive diarrhoea. Then the CBC narrows further: leucopenia points to enteric fever, eosinophilia to helminths. When both are absent, Shigella dysentery is the leading invasive cause."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well reasoned. Faecal leucocytes confirm mucosal invasion, and the CBC pattern helps exclude enteric fever and helminths — leaving Shigella as the most consistent diagnosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
