          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with confirmed intestinal amoebiasis has completed a full course of me</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with confirmed intestinal amoebiasis has completed a full course of metronidazole and is now asymptomatic. What is the essential next step in management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>No further treatment is required</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Stopping after metronidazole alone leaves viable cysts in the intestinal lumen. The patient may relapse and continues to transmit infection to others.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A course of albendazole</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Albendazole is an antihelminthic agent effective against roundworms and hookworms. It has no role in eliminating Entamoeba histolytica cysts.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A luminal agent such as diloxanide furoate</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. After a tissue amoebicide like metronidazole, a luminal agent (diloxanide furoate or paromomycin) is mandatory to eliminate intraluminal cyst carriage. This is the most commonly missed step in amoebiasis treatment.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Repeat colonoscopy after one month</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While follow-up may be warranted in complicated cases, the immediate priority is eliminating cyst carriage with a luminal agent to prevent relapse and community transmission.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Metronidazole kills trophozoites in tissue but does not reliably clear cysts from the intestinal lumen. A luminal agent like diloxanide furoate or paromomycin must follow to prevent relapse and ongoing transmission."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The two-step regimen — tissue amoebicide followed by a luminal agent — is essential. Missing the second step is the single most common error in amoebiasis management."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
